{
  "name": "How to Ensure Your Kitchen Floor is Truly Slip-Resistant",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Making sure your kitchen floor is genuinely slip-resistant involves a few key steps beyond just picking out a product. First, you've got to choose a flooring material that actually has appropriate surface traction for kitchen use. Don't just go by looks; research the material's slip resistance ratings. Next, it's crucial to use the correct underlayment and installation method. A poorly installed floor, even with a great material, can still create hazards. You'll want to make sure that transitions between different flooring types, grout lines, and edges are all smooth and don't create any trip or slip hazards. Finally, remember that ongoing cleaning methods can impact slip resistance. Using the wrong products or leaving residues can actually reduce the traction your floor provides. If your kitchen has tricky layouts, potential moisture issues, or accessibility needs, getting a professional involved can ensure every detail contributes to a truly safe and slip-resistant environment.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
